ksu: make selinux_hide fake status page report a consistent seqno/policyload

KSU's reset_avc_cache() advances /sys/fs/selinux/status ->sequence while the
real policyload and the AVC decision seqno (av_decision.seqno, which avd_init
stamps from latest_granting) stay put. An app reading the mmap page and cross-
checking it against selinux_check_access sees a "seqno split" -- a KernelSU
tell (Duck Detector flags exactly this). initialize_fake_status() memcpy()s the
real page, so the app-facing fake page inherits the split.

Set the fake page (apps only; uid>=10000+seccomp) to policyload = the live
policy seqno (ksu_selinux_policy_seqno() == latest_granting == the avd.seqno an
app reads) and sequence = 2*that. The pair is then consistent AND matches the
decision seqno. The real page is untouched, so early-boot userspace object
managers are not perturbed (the real-page spoof hung LineageOS boot).

diff --git a/kernel/feature/selinux_hide.c b/kernel/feature/selinux_hide.c
--- a/kernel/feature/selinux_hide.c
+++ b/kernel/feature/selinux_hide.c
@@ -116,4 +116,20 @@ static void initialize_fake_status(void)
 		new_status->sequence = 4;
 	}
+	/*
+	 * caymanslm: KSU reset_avc_cache() advanced ->sequence while the real
+	 * policyload and the AVC decision seqno stayed at latest_granting, so an
+	 * app reading this page (and cross-checking selinux_check_access) sees a
+	 * "seqno split". Present a consistent pair in the app-facing fake page
+	 * only: policyload = the live policy seqno (= the avd.seqno an app gets)
+	 * and sequence = 2*that. The real page is untouched so boot is safe.
+	 */
+	{
+		extern u32 ksu_selinux_policy_seqno(void);
+		u32 lg = ksu_selinux_policy_seqno();
+		if (lg) {
+			new_status->policyload = lg;
+			new_status->sequence = lg << 1;
+		}
+	}
 	
 	WRITE_ONCE(fake_status, new_page);
